  "worked_solution": "Using the power law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\n{({\\text{i}}^{3})}^{10}={\\text{i}}^{3\\cdot 10}={\\text{i}}^{30}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=30.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n30÷4=7\\,\\text{R}2.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{30}={\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.\n$$",
